Z||000002||bHooper||02-16-2001||09:58 AM||||me also, but for the 2.8 liter.||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||02-16-2001||11:30 AM||||as far as the 2.8 aftermarket throttle bodies are concerned I find it a bit rediculous spending so much money on something that is only milled out to another 3 mm, remeber that a motor is only a glorified air pump, the more air that is sucked in, the more exhaust that has to leave - so if you do happen to bolt on one of these throttle bodies, remember that you need to compensate for it by adding exhaust<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-16-2001||08:32 PM||||The large bore TBI will give a modest improvement on it's own, but there are other upgrades that will give you more power for your money.  <P>Porting the head/three angle vavle job, upgrading the ignition system, and aftermarket camshafts are good places to start.  If you have not already done so, get a new catalytic converter.  The stock ones are restrictive.  <P>After all of this stuff has been done, then changing to the big bore TBI will probably make a real difference.  Think of it as opening up the narrowest orifice in your intake/exhaust.  The TBI is not the most restrictive part until you fix a lot of other restrictions elsewhere.<P>Be careful!  The iron duke is good for about 120 to 140 HP and about 5500 rpms max.  There are a lot of horror stories in this forum about what happens when the weak crankshaft, rods, block, and head of the iron duke are overstressed.  Blowing a rod carries a significant risk of fire!<BR>Most will say that you should keep the duke mods as modest as you can.  <BR>So, how about:  <P>MSD ignition, Ported and polished head with three angle valve job, Big bore TBI, ported intake manifold to match TBI gasket, aftermarket catalytic converter, and V6 dual outlet exhaust (optional).<P>These mods should be good for at least 20 horsepower.  That's about 25% more than a stock duke, enough to reduce the power to weight ratio from about 1HP:27lb to about 1HP:20lb.  That translates to a drammatic improvement in performance.  This is my current goal as I "tweak" the duke in my 1984 automatic Fiero.  <P>If you want more than this, then it is time to get into a better block and crank.  Perhaps the 88 Astro van 2.5 duke would be sufficient to get handle 150 hp.  You will probably also want forged crank, rods, and pistons to get more stroke and compression.  A mild hydrallic roller cam, metal timing gears, and a high volume oil pump would complete this performance engine.  Remember that 150 HP is 50% more than stock.  <P>If 150 HP is not enough, then it's super duty time.  I think this means a $2200 racing block, extrude hone porting, header, holley high volume fuel pump and adjustable regulator, and a wild cam.  Idle speed will be a lumpy 1000 rpm or more. This is your barely-street-legal race motor.  BIG $$$$ <P>||||reg||1
